# Copy to .env.relay.local on the machine that runs tools/fivem-uplink.mjs.
#
# Why this file exists: the game server black-holes datacenter traffic, so the
# monitor reads it through a relay on a home machine. That relay publishes its
# tunnel URL to the `fivem_uplink` row of whatever Supabase project the
# deployment reads. Since scripts/split-to-prod.sh, the deployment reads a
# SEPARATE prod project — not the dev project .env.local points at — so the
# relay needs the prod URL + service key here.
#
# tools/fivem-uplink.mjs loads, highest priority first:
#   real env vars  ->  .env.relay.local  ->  .env.prod.local  ->  .env.local
# so this file only needs the two keys that differ from .env.local.
#
# .env.relay.local is git-ignored. It holds a service_role key (full DB access) —
# keep it only on the relay machine.

# ── the PROD Supabase project (Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API) ─────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://PRODREF.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="prod-service-role-or-secret-key"

# ── redundancy: run this on two machines ───────────────────────────────────
# Leave FIVEM_UPLINK_ROLE unset (or "primary") on the machine that should
# always publish. Set it to "standby" on the second machine — it keeps its
# relay + tunnel warm but only writes the row once the primary's heartbeat
# goes stale, and backs off again when the primary returns.
# FIVEM_UPLINK_ROLE="standby"
# FIVEM_STANDBY_TAKEOVER_MS="180000"   # stale-row grace before standby steps in

# ── optional relay tuning (defaults are fine) ───────────────────────────────
# FIVEM_RELAY_PORT="8787"
# FIVEM_HEARTBEAT_MS="60000"
# FIVEM_TUNNEL_URL="https://your-named-tunnel.example.com"  # skip cloudflared quick tunnel
